<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-getting-started" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Getting Started with Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Before we can start exploring data in R, there are some key concepts
            to understand first:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What is R-Instat?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        How do I use R-Instat?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        What are R-Instat packages?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <p>
            We'll introduce these concepts in the upcoming
            <xref ref="sec-r-rstudio" text="title"/> –
            <xref ref="sec-packages" text="title"/>.
            If you are already somewhat familiar with these concepts, feel free
            to skip to <xref ref="sec-nycflights"/> where we'll introduce our
            first dataset: all domestic flights departing one of the three main
            New York City (NYC) airports in 2023.
            We will explore this dataset in depth for much of the rest of this
            book.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-r-rstudio">
        <title>What is R-Instat?</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Throughout this book, we will assume that you are using
                R-Instat.
                If you wish to use R via RStudio then you can use
                <url href="https://pretextbooks.github.io/ModernDive_book">Modern Dive</url>
                which is the RStudio version of this book.
            </p>

            <p>
                R is a programming language that runs computations, whereas
                R-Instat is an <em>graphical user interface (GUI)</em> that
                provides an interface by adding many convenient features and
                tools.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="sec-install-rinstat">
            <title>Installing R-Instat</title>

            <p>
                Throughout this book we will use <term>R-Instat</term>, a free
                statistical software package built on top of the R programming
                language.
                R-Instat provides a user-friendly interface to many of R's
                statistical and graphical tools, making it possible to explore
                and analyse data without writing code.
            </p>

            <p>
                The latest version of R-Instat can be downloaded from the
                official website:
                <url href="https://r-instat.org">https://r-instat.org</url>.
                Choose the version that matches your operating system and follow
                the installation instructions.
            </p>

            <p>
                R-Instat includes its own copy of R, so for most users there is
                no need to install R separately.
                If you already have R installed on your computer, R-Instat will
                detect and use it automatically.
            </p>

            <p>
                After installation, start R-Instat.
                The opening screen provides quick access to common tasks,
                including importing data from files, importing example datasets
                from R packages, and opening previously saved R-Instat
                databooks.
                Throughout this book we will begin almost every activity from
                this starting screen.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-rinstat-start">
                <!-- Screenshot of the R-Instat start screen -->
                <caption>The R-Instat start screen.</caption>
            </figure>
        </subsection>
    </section>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-code">
        <title>How do I use R-Instat?</title>
        <p>To do</p>
    </section>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-nycflights">
        <title>Explore your first datasets</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let's put everything we've learned so far into practice and start
            exploring some real data!
            Data comes to us in a variety of formats, from pictures to text to
            numbers.
            Throughout this book, we'll focus on datasets that are saved in
            <q>spreadsheet</q>-type format.
            This is probably the most common way data are collected and saved in
            many fields.
            Remember from <xref ref="sec-programming-concepts"/> that these
            <q>spreadsheet</q>-type datasets are called <em>data frames</em> in
            R.
            <idx><h>data frame</h></idx> We'll focus on working with data saved
            as data frames throughout this book.
        </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-nycflights23-package">
            <title><c>nycflights23</c> package</title>

            <p>
                Many of us have flown on airplanes or know someone who has.
                Air travel has become an ever-present aspect of many people's
                lives.
                If you look at the Departures flight information board at an
                airport, you will frequently see that some flights are delayed
                for a variety of reasons.
                Are there ways that we can understand the reasons that cause
                flight delays?
            </p>

            <p>
                We'd all like to arrive at our destinations on time whenever
                possible.
                (Unless you secretly love hanging out at airports.
                If you are one of these people, pretend for a moment that you
                are very much anticipating being at your final destination.)
                Throughout this book, we're going to analyze data related to all
                domestic flights departing from one of New York City's three
                main airports in 2023: Newark Liberty International (EWR), John
                F.
                Kennedy International (JFK), and LaGuardia Airport (LGA).
                We'll access this data using the <c>nycflights23</c>
                <idx><h>R packages</h><h>nycflights23</h></idx> R package, which
                contains five datasets saved in five data frames:
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <c>flights</c>: Information on all flights.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <c>airlines</c>: A table matching airline names and
                            their two-letter International Air Transport
                            Association (IATA) airline codes (also known as
                            carrier codes) for 14 airline companies.
                            For example, <q>DL</q> is the two-letter code for
                            Delta.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <c>planes</c>: Information about each of the
                            physical aircraft used.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <c>weather</c>: Hourly meteorological data for each
                            of the three NYC airports.
                            This data frame has rows roughly corresponding to
                            the <m>365 \times 24 \times 3 = 26{,}280</m>
                            possible hourly measurements one can observe at
                            three locations over the course of a year.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <c>airports</c>: Names, codes, and locations of the
                            domestic destinations.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                The <c>nycflights23</c> package is an updated version of
                <url href="https://nycflights13.tidyverse.org/">the classic <c>nycflights13</c> R
        package</url>.
                <c>nycflights23</c> was authored by ModernDive co-author Chester
                Ismay using the
                <url href="https://anyflights.netlify.app/"><c>anyflights</c> R package</url>
                developed by
                <url href="https://www.simonpcouch.com/">Simon Couch</url>.
                Simon granted permission to the ModernDive team to create
                <c>nycflights23</c> and submit the package to CRAN.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-flights-data-frame">
            <title><c>flights</c> data frame</title>

            <p>
                We'll begin by exploring the <c>flights</c> data frame and get
                an idea of its structure.
                To do this, we first need to import the dataset into R-Instat.
            </p>

            <assemblage xml:id="howto-import-dataset">
                <title>How to Import a Dataset in R-Instat</title>

                <p>
                    Start <term>R-Instat</term>.
                    On the opening screen choose
                    <alert>File &gt; Import from Library</alert>.
                    This opens the dialog shown in
                    <xref ref="fig-import-library"/>.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-import-library">
                    <caption>Importing a dataset from an R package.</caption>
                    <image source="images/import-dlg-1.png" width="70%"/>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    Take a moment to explore the dialog before importing the
                    data.
                    Many R-Instat dialogs share a common structure that you will
                    see throughout this book.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            The option buttons at the top allow you to choose
                            different data libraries.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            The five buttons at the bottom appear on almost
                            every R-Instat dialog.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            The <alert>OK</alert> button is disabled until
                            enough information has been provided to complete the
                            task.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            The <alert>To Script</alert> button shows the R code
                            generated by the dialog.
                            We return to this in a later chapter.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            The <alert>Help</alert> button provides help on
                            using the dialog itself, while <alert>R Help</alert>
                            displays the documentation for the selected dataset.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Open the <alert>From Package</alert> drop-down list and
                    select the package <c>nycflights23</c>.
                    Then select the dataset <c>flights</c>.
                    Notice that the <alert>R Help</alert> button is now enabled.
                    If you click it, you can read the documentation describing
                    the dataset and its variables.
                    Close the help window when you are finished.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Press <alert>OK</alert> to import the data into R-Instat.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-flights-data">
                    <caption>
                        The <c>flights</c> data displayed in R-Instat.
                    </caption>

                    <image source="images/flights-data.png" width="70%"/>
                </figure>
            </assemblage>

            <p>
                The main R-Instat window displays the data in a spreadsheet-like
                format.
                Each row represents one observation and each column represents a
                variable.
                For the <c>flights</c> dataset, each observation corresponds to
                a single flight departing from one of New York City's airports
                during 2023.
            </p>

            <p>
                The variable names appear at the top of each column, while the
                row numbers appear down the left-hand side.
                At the bottom of the data window, R-Instat reports the number of
                rows and columns in the data frame.
                You should confirm that the dataset contains 435,352 rows and 19
                columns.
            </p>

            <p>
                Let's look at some different ways to explore this data frames.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-exploredataframes">
            <title>Exploring data frames</title>

            <p>
                There are many ways to get a feel for the data contained in a
                data frame such as <c>flights</c>.
                We present XXX ways that this can be done in R-Instat:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Viewing the Data in the R-Instat "Data View"
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p/>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

            <subsubsection>
                <title>Viewing the Data</title>

                <p>
                    By looking at the View Data window, we can explore the
                    different <em>variables</em> listed in the columns.
                    Observe that there are many different types of variables.
                    Some of the variables like <c>distance</c>, <c>day</c>, and
                    <c>arr_delay</c> are what we will call <em>quantitative</em>
                    variables.
                    <idx><h>quantitative</h></idx> These variables are numerical
                    in nature.
                    Other variables here are <idx><h>categorical</h></idx>
                    <em>categorical</em>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If you look in the leftmost column of the Data View output,
                    you'll see a column of numbers.
                    These are the row numbers of the dataset.
                    Glancing across a row with the same number, say row 5, you
                    can get an idea of what each row represents.
                    This allows you to identify what object is being described
                    in a given row by taking note of the values of the columns
                    in that specific row.
                    This is often called the <em>observational unit</em>.
                    <idx><h>observational unit</h></idx> The observational unit
                    in this example is an individual flight departing from New
                    York City in 2023.
                    You can identify the observational unit by determining what
                    <q>thing</q> is being measured or described by each of the
                    variables.
                    We'll talk more about observational units in
                    <xref ref="subsec-identification-vs-measurement-variables"/>
                    on <em>identification</em> and <em>measurement</em>
                    variables.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The <em>data type</em> (see
                    <xref ref="sec-programming-concepts"/> ) of the variable is
                    given immediately after each variable's name in the data
                    viewer.
                    If there is no letter symbol, then it is a numerical
                    variable.
                </p>

                <p>
                    In contrast, <c>C</c> refers to <q>character,</q> which is
                    computer terminology for text data.
                    In most forms, text data, such as the <c>carrier</c> or
                    <c>origin</c> of a flight, are categorical variables.
                    The <c>time_hour</c> variable is another data type:
                    <c>D.T</c>.
                    These types of variables represent date and time
                    combinations.
                    However, we won't work with dates and times in this book; we
                    leave this topic for other data science books like
                    <url href="https://datasciencebook.ca/"><em>Data Science: A First Introduction</em>
        by Tiffany-Anne Timbers, Melissa Lee, and Trevor Campbell</url>
                    or
                    <url href="https://r4ds.had.co.nz/dates-and-times.html"><em>R for Data
        Science</em></url>
                    <xref ref="bib-rds2016"/>.
                </p>

                <exercise xml:id="lc1-4">
                    <title>Learning Check</title>

                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            (LC1.4) What are some other examples in this dataset
                            of <em>categorical</em> variables?
                            What makes them different than <em>quantitative</em>
                            variables?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>
            </subsubsection>

            <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-visualise-data">
                <title>Understanding the structure of the data</title>

                <p>
                    Rather than inspecting every column individually, R-Instat
                    can provide a quick overview of the whole dataset.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Choose
                    <alert>Describe &gt; One Variable &gt; Visualise Data</alert>
                    and press <alert>OK</alert> without changing any options.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-visualise-flights">
                    <!-- screenshot -->
                    <caption>The Visualise Data summary.</caption>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    This display provides an overview of the dataset.
                    Different colours indicate different variable types, while
                    grey regions highlight missing values.
                    You can immediately see which variables are numerical, which
                    contain text, and whether any variables contain missing
                    observations.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This type of overview is often the quickest way to develop
                    an understanding of an unfamiliar dataset before beginning a
                    detailed analysis.
                </p>
            </subsubsection>

            <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-summarise-data">
                <title>Summarising variables</title>

                <p>
                    A second useful tool is the
                    <alert>Describe &gt; One Variable &gt;
Summarise</alert>
                    dialog.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Add all the variables and press <alert>OK</alert>.
                    The Output window displays appropriate summaries for every
                    variable in the dataset.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-summary-flights">
                    <!-- screenshot -->
                    <caption>
                        Summary of the variables in the <c>flights</c> dataset.
                    </caption>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    Notice that R-Instat chooses summaries appropriate to each
                    type of variable.
                    Numerical variables are summarised using statistics such as
                    the minimum, median and maximum values, while categorical
                    variables are summarised using their frequencies.
                    This allows you to quickly identify variables with many
                    categories, missing values, or unexpected patterns.
                </p>

                <exercise xml:id="lc1-3">
                    <title>Learning Check</title>

                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            (LC1.3) What does any <em>ONE</em> row in this
                            <c>flights</c> dataset refer to?
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>A. Data on an airline</li>
                                <li>B. Data on a flight</li>
                                <li>C. Data on an airport</li>
                                <li>D. Data on multiple flights</li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </exercise>
            </subsubsection>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="sec-identification-variables">
            <title>Identification and measurement variables</title>

            <p>
                There is a subtle difference between the kinds of variables that
                you will encounter in data frames.
                There are <em>identification</em> variables and
                <em>measurement</em> variables.
                For example, let's explore the <c>airports</c> data frame
            </p>

            <p>
                The variables <c>faa</c> and <c>name</c> are
                <em>identification variables</em> that uniquely identify each
                airport.
                <c>faa</c> provides the airport’s unique FAA code, while
                <c>name</c> gives its official name.
                These variables are used to uniquely identify each row in a data
                frame.
                The remaining variables ( <c>lat</c>, <c>lon</c>, <c>alt</c>,
                <c>tz</c>, <c>dst</c>, <c>tzone</c> ) are often called
                <em>measurement</em> or <em>characteristic</em> variables:
                variables that describe properties of each observational unit.
                For example, <c>lat</c> and <c>lon</c> describe the latitude and
                longitude of each airport.
            </p>

            <p>
                Furthermore, sometimes a single variable might not be enough to
                uniquely identify each observational unit: combinations of
                variables might be needed.
                While it is not an absolute rule, for organizational purposes it
                is considered good practice to have your identification
                variables in the leftmost columns of your data frame.
            </p>

            <exercise xml:id="lc1-5">
                <title>Learning Check</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        (LC1.5) What properties of each airport do the variables
                        <c>lat</c>, <c>lon</c>, <c>alt</c>, <c>tz</c>,
                        <c>dst</c>, and <c>tzone</c> describe in the
                        <c>airports</c> data frame?
                        Take your best guess.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="lc1-6">
                <title>Learning Check</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        (LC1.6) Provide the names of variables in a data frame
                        with at least three variables where one of them is an
                        identification variable and the other two are not.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-help-files">
            <title>Help files</title>

            <p>
                Another nice feature of R-Instat are help files, which provide
                documentation for various functions and datasets.
                You can bring up help files by clicking the "help" button on a
                dialog.
                You will then be presented with a page showing the corresponding
                documentation.
                For example, we saw in TODO how to look at the help file for the
                <c>flights</c> data frame.
            </p>

            <p>
                Whenever you import a dataset from an R package, the
                <alert>R Help</alert> button displays the documentation supplied
                with that dataset.
                This documentation explains where the data came from and
                describes each variable.
            </p>

            <p>
                Most dialogs also include a <alert>Help</alert> button that
                opens the R-Instat help system.
                These pages explain the purpose of the dialog, describe its
                options, and often include worked examples.
                If you are unsure how a dialog works, consulting the help page
                is usually the quickest way to learn more.
            </p>

            <exercise xml:id="lc1-7">
                <title>Learning Check</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        (LC1.7) Look at the help file for the <c>airports</c>
                        data frame.
                        Revise your earlier guesses about what the variables
                        <c>lat</c>, <c>lon</c>, <c>alt</c>, <c>tz</c>,
                        <c>dst</c>, and <c>tzone</c> each describe.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
        </subsection>
    </section>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-conclusion-ch1">
        <title>Conclusion</title>

        <p>
            This chapter provides a small set of tools to explore data in R, but
            it's far from exhaustive.
            Including everything would overwhelm rather than help.
            The best way to add to your toolbox is running and writing code in
            RStudio as much as possible.
        </p>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-additional-resources">
            <title>Additional resources</title>

            <p>
                If you are new to the world of R-Instat and feel you could
                benefit from a more detailed introduction, we suggest the moodle
                page.
                It includes videos and tutorials that you can follow along and
                pause as you learn.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-whats-to-come">
            <title>What's to come?</title>

            <p>
                We're next heading into the
                <q>Data Science with <c>tidyverse</c></q> portion in
                <xref ref="ch-visualization"/> as shown in
                <xref ref="fig-moderndive-flowchart"/> with what we feel is the
                most important tool in a data scientist's toolbox: data
                visualization.
                We'll continue to explore the data included in the
                <c>moderndive</c> and <c>nycflights23</c> packages using the
                <c>ggplot2</c> package for data visualization.
                Data visualization is a powerful tool to add to your toolbox for
                data exploration that provides additional insight to what the
                describe dialogs we have looked at so far can provide.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-moderndive-flowchart">
                <caption><em>ModernDive</em> flowchart – on to Part I!</caption>
                <image source="images/flowcharts/flowchart/flowchart.004.png" width="70%">
                    <description>
                        A flowchart showing the structure of the ModernDive
                        book, with Chapter 1 completed and an arrow pointing to
                        Part I: Data Science with tidyverse.
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </subsection>
    </section>
</chapter>